# Controller Implementation Patterns

## Architecture Overview

Controllers in this project follow the **Service Layer Pattern** to ensure clean separation of concerns and enable MCP compatibility. Controllers handle HTTP-specific logic while delegating business logic to services.

## Key Principles

### 1. Service Layer Delegation
Controllers should **never** contain business logic. All data processing, validation, and business rules belong in the service layer.

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT - Delegates to service
const result = await issueService.getAllIssues(params);

// ❌ WRONG - Business logic in controller
const issues = await issueModel.findMany(filters, page, limit);
for (const issue of issues) { /* processing */ }
```

### 2. HTTP-Only Concerns
Controllers should only handle:
- Parameter extraction from `req.query`, `req.params`, `req.body`
- HTTP status codes and responses
- Error handling with appropriate HTTP error codes

## Standard Controller Pattern

### Reference Implementation
See [src/controllers/issueController.js](mdc:src/controllers/issueController.js) for the correct pattern.

### Template Structure
```javascript
import { domainService } from "../services/domainService.js";

export const domainController = {
  // GET /domain - List with filtering/pagination
  getAll: async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const { filter1, filter2, page = 1, limit = 30 } = req.query;
      
      const result = await domainService.getAll({
        filter1,
        filter2,
        page,
        limit,
      });

      return res.status(200).json(result);
    } catch (error) {
      return res.status(500).json({
        error: `Failed to fetch items. ${error.message}`,
      });
    }
  },

  // GET /domain/:id - Get by ID
  getById: async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const { id } = req.params;
      const item = await domainService.getById(id);
      return res.status(200).json(item);
    } catch (error) {
      return res.status(404).json({
        error: error.message,
      });
    }
  },

  // POST /domain - Create new item
  create: async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const itemData = req.body;
      const item = await domainService.create(itemData);
      return res.status(201).json(item);
    } catch (error) {
      return res.status(400).json({
        error: error.message,
      });
    }
  },

  // PATCH/PUT /domain/:id - Update item
  update: async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const { id } = req.params;
      const updateData = req.body;
      
      const updatedItem = await domainService.update(id, updateData);
      return res.status(200).json(updatedItem);
    } catch (error) {
      return res.status(400).json({
        error: `Failed to update item. ${error.message}`,
      });
    }
  },
};
```

## Error Handling Standards

### HTTP Status Code Guidelines
- **200**: Successful GET, PATCH/PUT operations
- **201**: Successful POST (creation)
- **400**: Bad request (validation errors, malformed data)
- **404**: Resource not found (typically for GET/UPDATE by ID)
- **500**: Internal server error (unexpected failures)

### Error Response Format
```javascript
// Consistent error response structure
return res.status(statusCode).json({
  error: error.message || "Default error message",
});
```

### Error Handling Pattern
```javascript
try {
  // Controller logic here
  const result = await service.operation(params);
  return res.status(200).json(result);
} catch (error) {
  // Log if needed for debugging
  console.error("Operation failed:", error);
  
  // Return appropriate HTTP error
  return res.status(appropriateCode).json({
    error: `Failed to perform operation. ${error.message}`,
  });
}
```

## Parameter Extraction Patterns

### Query Parameters (GET requests)
```javascript
// With defaults for pagination
const { category, name, page = 1, limit = 30 } = req.query;

// Optional filters
const { status, environment, from, to } = req.query;
```

### Path Parameters
```javascript
// Single ID parameter
const { issueId } = req.params;
const { resultId } = req.params;

// Multiple path parameters
const { userId, projectId } = req.params;
```

### Request Body
```javascript
// For POST/PUT/PATCH operations
const itemData = req.body;
const updateData = req.body;

// With destructuring if needed
const { name, category, description } = req.body;
```

## MCP Compatibility Benefits

This pattern enables MCP compatibility because:

1. **Shared Business Logic**: Services can be used by both HTTP controllers and MCP handlers
2. **Consistent Validation**: Same validation logic in services for both transport methods
3. **Single Source of Truth**: Business rules exist in one place (services)
4. **Easy Testing**: Services can be tested independently of transport layer

### Example MCP Integration
```javascript
// HTTP Controller (src/controllers/issueController.js)
const result = await issueService.getAllIssues(params);
return res.status(200).json(result);

// MCP Handler (src/handlers/mcpIssueHandler.js)  
const result = await issueService.getAllIssues(params);
return result; // No HTTP concerns
```

## Refactoring Existing Controllers

### Before (Anti-pattern)
```javascript
export const oldController = {
  getItems: async (req, res) => {
    const { filter } = req.query;
    
    // ❌ Business logic in controller
    const items = await itemModel.findMany(filter);
    const processed = items.map(item => ({
      ...item,
      calculated: item.value * 2
    }));
    
    return res.json(processed);
  }
};
```

### After (Correct pattern)
```javascript
export const newController = {
  getItems: async (req, res) => {
    try {
      const { filter } = req.query;
      
      // ✅ Delegate to service
      const items = await itemService.getItems({ filter });
      return res.status(200).json(items);
    } catch (error) {
      return res.status(500).json({
        error: `Failed to fetch items. ${error.message}`,
      });
    }
  }
};
```

## Common Patterns

### List with Pagination
```javascript
getAllItems: async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { category, status, page = 1, limit = 30 } = req.query;
    
    const result = await itemService.getAll({
      category,
      status,
      page: Number(page),
      limit: Number(limit),
    });

    return res.status(200).json(result);
  } catch (error) {
    return res.status(500).json({
      error: `Failed to fetch items. ${error.message}`,
    });
  }
},
```

### Get by ID with 404 handling
```javascript
getItemById: async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const { itemId } = req.params;
    const item = await itemService.getById(itemId);
    return res.status(200).json(item);
  } catch (error) {
    // Service should throw meaningful errors
    return res.status(404).json({
      error: error.message,
    });
  }
},
```

### Create with 201 status
```javascript
createItem: async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const itemData = req.body;
    const item = await itemService.create(itemData);
    return res.status(201).json(item);
  } catch (error) {
    return res.status(400).json({
      error: error.message,
    });
  }
},
```

## Testing Controllers

Controllers should be tested to verify:
- Correct parameter extraction
- Proper service method calls
- Appropriate HTTP status codes
- Error handling scenarios

## Service Layer Requirements

When creating controllers, ensure corresponding services exist in [src/services/](mdc:src/services) following the service layer patterns.

This architecture ensures your controllers are clean, testable, and enable seamless MCP integration.

